LESSONS_S5 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Delivery Note: scope and position", "11", "<p>This module covers the Delivery Note and sales returns in ZhiftERP. On completing this module you will be able to: complete the Delivery Note field walk; run the picking and despatch procedure with its verification step; execute partial and wave deliveries against order rows; state what submission posts to stock and accounting; process returns through the return lane with the condition decision; and run the delivery reads.</p><p><b>What a Delivery Note is.</b> The Delivery Note records a physical despatch of goods to a customer: which items, in what quantities, from which warehouse, to which address, on which date. It is the document of custody transfer — before submission the goods are the business's stock; after submission they are the customer's goods — and it is the delivery evidence every later question refers to: what shipped, when, with which serial and batch numbers, and who signed for it.</p><p><b>Position in the flow.</b> The Delivery Note sits between the Sales Order and the Sales Invoice:</p><p>1. <b>It is created from the order</b> — the order states the commitment; the Delivery Note records the portion of it performed in this despatch. Direct Delivery Notes without orders are permitted only where the policy allows them (routine trade per the site's configuration); the standing pattern is order first.<br>2. <b>It advances %delivered</b> — the order's fulfilment figure computes from submitted Delivery Notes.<br>3. <b>It feeds invoicing</b> — module 7 bills delivered quantities per the payment schedule; the delivered-not-billed read (module 4, Chapter 7) measures the gap.</p><p><b>What submission does, stated in advance.</b> Chapter 5 covers the postings in detail. In summary: stock leaves the warehouse (Stock Ledger Entries), cost of goods sold posts under perpetual inventory, and the reserved quantity for the delivered portion releases. A submitted Delivery Note is therefore a stock and accounting event, and the disciplines in this module exist to ensure the event matches the physical despatch exactly.</p><p><b>Custody.</b> The warehouse function picks and despatches; a second person verifies the picked goods against the document before submission; the sales function monitors fulfilment through the reads; and the standing rule for the module: <b>the Delivery Note states what physically left, with its identity, verified before submission and acknowledged at receipt.</b></p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the field walk. Chapter 3: picking and despatch. Chapter 4: partial and wave deliveries. Chapter 5: what submission posts. Chapter 6: returns. Chapter 7: the delivery reads. Chapter 8: worked example. Chapter 9: review and the module rule.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Delivery Note doctype", "12", "<p>Create the Delivery Note from the Sales Order (Create &gt; Delivery Note) so the rows, rates, and references carry over. Complete or verify the fields in order.</p><p><b>1. Customer and addresses.</b> The customer carries from the order. Verify two addresses:</p><p>1. <b>The shipping address</b> — the destination of this despatch. On multi-site customers (module 1, Chapter 4) each Delivery Note selects the receiving branch's address; the address prints on the despatch paperwork the driver carries.<br>2. <b>The billing address</b> — carried for the invoice linkage; it does not change per despatch.</p><p><b>2. Posting Date and Time.</b> The date and time the goods physically leave. The honest-date rule from the Inventory track applies: the Delivery Note posts when the despatch happens, on the date it happens.</p><p><b>3. The items table.</b> Rows draw from the order:</p><p>1. <b>Quantity per row</b> — the quantity in this despatch, up to the row's remaining undelivered quantity. Chapter 4 covers partial rows.<br>2. <b>UOM</b> — as ordered; the unit column is read per the standing discipline.<br>3. <b>Source Warehouse</b> — the warehouse the goods leave. It must be the warehouse the goods physically leave; a Delivery Note posting from one warehouse while goods leave another corrupts both warehouses' balances.<br>4. <b>Serial and batch numbers</b> — controlled items carry their identity on the rows through the bundle (Inventory track, module 3): the delivered batch and serial numbers are the record the recall trace, the warranty file, and any later dispute read from.<br>5. <b>Rate and amount</b> — carried from the order for the invoice linkage; the Delivery Note does not renegotiate prices.</p><p><b>4. Transporter details.</b> Where the site uses them: the transporter, vehicle, and driver fields per the despatch procedure. These fields identify the carrier leg for the transit-damage read (Chapter 7).</p><p><b>5. Order reference.</b> The linked Sales Order per row — created-from carries it. The reference is what advances %delivered and what the invoice will draw on.</p><p><b>6. Save, verify, submit.</b> The Delivery Note is saved as a draft for the verification step (Chapter 3), and submitted at the moment of despatch. Submission posts the stock and accounting events (Chapter 5); after submission, corrections follow the document rules — cancel and amend with cause, or the return lane where goods have physically moved (Chapter 6).</p><p><b>Verification per document.</b> The shipping address is the destination; the posting date is the despatch date; each row's quantity, unit, and warehouse match the physical goods; identity numbers are complete on controlled rows; the order reference is present.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Picking and despatch", "12", "<p>The procedure between the planning read and the departing vehicle. Each step has an owner and a record.</p><p><b>1. The pick list.</b> The weekly planning read (module 4, Chapter 4) provides the rows due in the window. Per despatch, the pick list states: the order reference, the items, the quantities in their units, the source warehouse locations, and the identity requirements for controlled items. The picker works from the list, not from memory of the order.</p><p><b>2. Picking.</b></p><p>1. <b>Pick against the list</b> — item, quantity, unit, per line; part-quantities counted in units per the unit-column discipline.<br>2. <b>Capture identity at pick</b> — for batch-controlled items, the picked batch is the batch recorded (first-expiry-first-out per the Inventory track's picking rule); for serialised items, the physical serial numbers of the picked units are scanned or recorded. The identity on the document must be the identity in the carton; the Inventory track's rule that the document names what was physically picked applies here in full.<br>3. <b>Stage the picked goods</b> — at the despatch area (the dispatch-bay warehouse where the site uses one), separated per destination.</p><p><b>3. The verification step.</b> Before submission, a second person checks the staged goods against the draft Delivery Note: line count, quantities in the stated units, identity numbers against the physical labels, destination address against the load plan. The check is recorded (the checker's confirmation per the site's procedure). This step exists because the Delivery Note becomes the custody and accounting record at submission, and an error caught at the bay costs minutes while an error discovered at the customer costs a return cycle.</p><p><b>4. Despatch and submission.</b> The Delivery Note submits when the goods physically leave — the same-time standard. The despatch pack travels with the goods: the printed Delivery Note (the site's standard format), and the documents the destination requires. For inter-city despatches the transporter fields are completed before departure.</p><p><b>5. The acknowledgment.</b> The customer's receipt is recorded:</p><p>1. <b>The signed copy</b> — the customer (or the receiving branch) signs the Delivery Note copy; the signed copy or its scan attaches to the document as the proof of delivery.<br>2. <b>Discrepancies at receipt</b> — the customer's count differing from the document is recorded on the acknowledgment at the point of delivery, and the discrepancy routes to the corrective lane the same day: the short line investigated (the pick error, the transit loss) and corrected by its proper document, never by an informal adjustment.<br>3. <b>The pending-acknowledgment read</b> (Chapter 7) lists deliveries without signed copies; the target is a complete file, because the acknowledgment is the document that settles a delivery dispute.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Partial and wave deliveries", "12", "<p>Orders fulfil in parts. This chapter covers the mechanics of partial rows, wave execution, and the tolerance settings.</p><p><b>1. Partial rows.</b> A Delivery Note row may carry less than the order row's quantity:</p><p>1. The delivered quantity posts; the remainder stays open on the order row with its date.<br>2. %delivered advances by the delivered portion.<br>3. Subsequent Delivery Notes draw the remaining quantity until the row completes.</p><p>Partial delivery is a normal mechanism, not an exception — the schedule's waves (module 4) and stock timing both use it. The requirement is that each partial despatch is a complete document in itself: verified, identified, acknowledged.</p><p><b>2. Wave execution.</b> A multi-wave order (per-row delivery dates, module 4, Chapter 4) executes as one Delivery Note per despatch event:</p><p>1. <b>One Delivery Note per vehicle-load per destination</b> — the same rule as internal transfers (Inventory track, module 2): the receiving count matches one physical event.<br>2. <b>The wave's rows only</b> — the Delivery Note draws the rows and quantities due in this wave; later waves' rows remain on the order with their dates.<br>3. <b>Multi-destination waves</b> — a wave serving several branches produces one Delivery Note per branch, each with its own shipping address, verification, and acknowledgment. The order remains the single commitment reference across all of them.</p><p><b>3. Over-delivery tolerance.</b> Stock Settings and the selling configuration carry the over-delivery allowance: the percentage by which a delivered quantity may exceed the ordered quantity before the system refuses the row. The standing posture is a low tolerance with the exception role named (the Inventory track, module 8 records the setting in the control register). The tolerance exists for genuine packing arithmetic (the carton that rounds a quantity), not as a licence to ship unordered goods; deliveries beyond it require the order amended first, through module 4's amendment lanes.</p><p><b>4. Delivery against sufficient stock.</b> The Delivery Note posts from actual stock. With Allow Negative Stock off (the standing posture), a despatch the warehouse balance cannot cover does not submit — which surfaces, at despatch time, either a picking error (the goods came from a different warehouse than the document states) or a records error (a movement was never posted). Both are corrected by their proper documents before the delivery proceeds; the refusal is the control operating as designed.</p><p><b>5. The completion of a row and an order.</b> A row completes when its delivered quantity equals its ordered quantity (within tolerance). An order completes when all rows complete and billing completes (module 7). Rows the customer will no longer take are closed through module 4's amendment or closure procedures — they do not remain open indefinitely, per the stale-order hygiene read.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — What submission posts", "12", "<p>Submission converts the verified document into stock and accounting events. This chapter states each posting and its consequence.</p><p><b>1. The stock posting.</b> Per row, a Stock Ledger Entry: the quantity leaves the source warehouse at the item's valuation rate (the Inventory track, module 5 — the method's rate, never a typed rate). Consequences:</p><p>1. The warehouse balance falls; the Bin updates.<br>2. For controlled items, the batch and serial balances fall per the bundle — the delivered identities are recorded as delivered, which is what makes the downstream recall trace and the warranty record possible.<br>3. The stock is no longer the business's: the custody transfer is effective at submission.</p><p><b>2. The accounting posting.</b> Under perpetual inventory: the stock account credits and cost of goods sold debits, at the valuation the stock ledger computed. This is the cost side of the sale's margin; the revenue side posts when the Sales Invoice submits (module 7). The two postings are deliberately separate documents because delivery and invoicing are separate events with separate dates, and the delivered-not-billed read measures the interval between them.</p><p><b>3. The reservation release.</b> The delivered quantity leaves Reserved Qty on the Bin: the commitment recorded at order submission (module 4, Chapter 3) is performed to that extent, and projected quantities update accordingly.</p><p><b>4. The order update.</b> %delivered advances on the linked order; the row's remaining quantity reduces; the order's status moves per the status vocabulary when thresholds are reached.</p><p><b>5. Corrections after submission.</b> The correction lane depends on whether goods physically moved:</p><p>1. <b>Goods did not leave</b> (the despatch was cancelled after submission): the Delivery Note cancels with its reason; the stock and accounting postings reverse; the order rows reopen. The cancellation is same-day, because a submitted Delivery Note for goods still on the shelf misstates both custody and cost for as long as it stands.<br>2. <b>Goods left and must come back:</b> the return lane (Chapter 6) — a new document recording the physical return, never a cancellation of the despatch that genuinely happened.<br>3. <b>A field error on a correct despatch</b> (a wrong reference, a missing transporter detail): the administrative correction per the document rules, with the note.</p><p><b>6. The posting verification.</b> Riding the Inventory track's monthly tie-out: Delivery Note postings are part of the stock-to-accounts agreement, and a tie-out difference traced to a delivery is investigated through this chapter's postings — the stock ledger row, the accounting entry, and the document, read together.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Returns", "13", "<p>Goods come back. This chapter is the return lane: the document, the condition decision, and the commercial tie.</p><p><b>1. The return Delivery Note.</b> The return is recorded on a Delivery Note marked as a return (Is Return), created from the original Delivery Note so the rows, rates, and references carry:</p><p>1. <b>The returned rows and quantities</b> — what physically came back, counted at receipt against the customer's return advice.<br>2. <b>The identity confirmed</b> — batch and serial numbers on the returned rows checked against the original delivery's numbers: the business confirms it is receiving back what it shipped. A returned serial that was never delivered is a stopped line and an investigation.<br>3. <b>The reason code</b> — from the defined set (damaged in transit, fault, wrong item, customer cancelled, near-expiry per agreement): the quarterly returns read aggregates by these codes.<br>4. <b>Submission posts the reverse movements</b> — stock re-enters the receiving warehouse; the accounting reverses cost per the valuation rules; %delivered adjusts on the order.</p><p><b>2. The condition decision.</b> Returned goods do not re-enter saleable stock automatically. At receipt, the condition decision routes the goods:</p><p>1. <b>Saleable</b> — undamaged, in date, sealed per the policy's saleability criteria: received into the trading warehouse.<br>2. <b>Quarantine</b> — damaged, suspect, opened, or requiring inspection: received into the quarantine warehouse (the Inventory track, module 1), and resolved from there through the inspection outcome — restored, written off through the damage lane, or returned to the supplier where the fault is upstream.<br>3. <b>The pharmaceutical rule</b> — returned pharmaceutical goods follow the compliance owner's saleability ruling; where regulation or policy prohibits resale of returned product classes, the quarantine-and-disposal route is the standing decision and the return's pricing reflects it.</p><p><b>3. The commercial tie.</b> The return's money side is module 7's territory, summarised here for the flow: the credit note (the return Sales Invoice) issues against the returned quantities per the policy — full credit, replacement, or repair per the case — and the customer's balance and statement reflect it. The rule at the boundary: <b>the stock document records what came back; the credit document records what the business owes for it</b> — two documents, each stating its own event, linked.</p><p><b>4. The return window and authorisation.</b> The policy states the return window per product class and the authorisation required: returns inside the window per the standard lane; returns beyond it as exceptions with named approval; returns of non-returnable classes refused with the policy reference. The authorisation is recorded on the return document, and the monthly returns read lists exceptions with their approvers.</p><p><b>5. Replacements.</b> Where the resolution is replacement rather than credit: the replacement ships as a new Delivery Note against the order or against the replacement procedure the site uses, with its own verification and identity capture; the faulty unit's return and the replacement's despatch are two documents whose references link them. Warranty-covered replacements additionally update the serial record's warranty history (the Inventory track, module 3).</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The delivery reads", "12", "<p>The standing reads on the delivery function, each with its cadence and action.</p><p><b>1. The pending-acknowledgment read — weekly.</b> Submitted Delivery Notes without an attached signed copy, by age. Each is chased to its proof of delivery; the file's completeness is the measure. An unacknowledged delivery is an unproven custody transfer, and the read keeps the exposure short.</p><p><b>2. The delivered-not-billed read — weekly.</b> Module 4's read, operated from the delivery side: submitted deliveries whose invoicing has not followed within the cadence. The list feeds module 7's invoicing run and clears at each run.</p><p><b>3. The overdue and at-risk rows — weekly.</b> Module 4's planning read continues through execution: rows past date, rows due with short stock — each with owner and action.</p><p><b>4. The returns read — monthly.</b> The month's returns by reason code, customer, item class, and value:</p><p>1. <b>The reason distribution</b> — transit damage rising routes to the carrier and packing review; wrong-item returns route to the picking procedure; fault returns route to the supplier quality lane (the Procurement track).<br>2. <b>The exception list</b> — beyond-window and non-standard returns with their approvers.<br>3. <b>The condition outcomes</b> — returned value by route (saleable, quarantine, write-off): the figure that states what returns actually cost.</p><p><b>5. The transit-damage read — quarterly.</b> Damage-coded returns and delivery discrepancies grouped by transporter and route, from the Delivery Note transporter fields. The output is data for the carrier review: rates per carrier per route, with the documents referenced. Findings route to the despatch procedure (packing), the carrier conversation (performance), or both.</p><p><b>6. The despatch verification sample — monthly.</b> A sample of the month's Delivery Notes checked for the procedure's evidence: the verification step recorded, identity complete on controlled rows, acknowledgments attached, posting dates matching despatch dates. The sample is the procedure's audit; findings correct the procedure or the training, per the standing pattern, and the sample's results file with the month's read outputs.</p><p><b>7. The filing.</b> The delivery file per order — Delivery Notes, acknowledgments, discrepancy notes, returns and their resolutions — accumulates on the order file module 4 opened, keeping the one-lookup standard: any delivery question answers from the order's file.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Worked example: Montaigne wave one", "13", "<p>This chapter executes the first wave of the module 4 order. Values and outcomes are listed per step.</p><p><b>The wave.</b> Wave one of the Montaigne AH rollout covers the first <b>five branches</b>: each receives <b>1 terminal, 1 scanner, and 1 printer</b>. The order's per-row dates place the wave in the current week; the equipment arrived from the supplier inside the lead time and sits at the fulfilling warehouse with serials captured at receipt (the Procurement and Inventory tracks' receiving discipline).</p><p><b>Step 1 — the pick lists (Chapter 3).</b> The planning read presents the wave; despatch is planned as one vehicle serving the five branches on one route. Five pick lists issue — one per branch — each listing the three items with serial-capture requirements.</p><p><b>Step 2 — five Delivery Notes (Chapters 2, 4).</b> Per the one-document-per-destination rule, <b>five Delivery Notes</b> create from the order — one per branch, each carrying: the branch's shipping address (module 1's address set, titled by Montaigne's own branch codes), the three rows with quantity 1 each, and the picked units' <b>serial numbers</b> recorded at pick. Rates carry from the order (the 4% terminal concession intact on the terminal rows — the Delivery Note does not renegotiate prices).</p><p><b>Step 3 — verification and despatch (Chapter 3).</b> The staged sets are checked by the second person: fifteen units against fifteen document rows, serials against labels, addresses against the load plan. The five documents submit as the vehicle loads; the despatch packs travel; the transporter fields identify the carrier leg.</p><p><b>Step 4 — the postings (Chapter 5).</b> Each submission posts its stock ledger entries at valuation (the equipment's landed cost from the receiving flow), cost of goods sold under perpetual inventory, and the reservation release for the delivered units. %delivered on the order advances to the wave's proportion; the delivery-portion milestone (the 40% schedule row) becomes billable for the delivered branches, and module 7's invoicing run will draw it.</p><p><b>Step 5 — acknowledgments (Chapter 3).</b> Each branch signs its copy; the five scans attach. One branch's acknowledgment records a discrepancy: the printer carton shows transit damage. The discrepancy routes the same day.</p><p><b>Step 6 — the return lane runs (Chapter 6).</b> The damaged printer returns on a return Delivery Note created from the branch's original document: one row, quantity 1, the unit's serial confirmed against the delivered serial, reason code <i>damaged in transit</i>. The condition decision routes the unit to quarantine; the inspection confirms the damage; the supplier-quality lane takes the unit (the fault is upstream). A replacement ships as a new Delivery Note with its own serial and acknowledgment, the two documents cross-referenced. The transit-damage read records the incident against the carrier and route.</p><p><b>The wave closes.</b> Five deliveries acknowledged, one return resolved by replacement, the order file carrying the complete wave: five Delivery Notes, five acknowledgments, one return pair. Waves two and three remain on the order with their dates, and the planning read will present them in their weeks.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common errors and the module rule", "11", "<p>The errors that recur in delivery operations, each with its correction and prevention.</p><p><b>Error 1 — the document that does not match the despatch.</b> Symptom: quantities, warehouses, or identities on the Delivery Note differing from the physical load; both the customer's receipt and the stock records inheriting the difference. Correction: the discrepancy traced and corrected by the proper documents. Prevention: picking against the list, identity captured at pick, and the second-person verification before submission.</p><p><b>Error 2 — the unsubmitted despatch.</b> Symptom: goods leaving with the Delivery Note still in draft; stock records showing goods the shelf no longer holds; custody transferred with no record. Correction: the document submitted with the honest despatch date and the gap noted. Prevention: submission at the moment of despatch as the standing procedure.</p><p><b>Error 3 — the missing acknowledgment.</b> Symptom: deliveries without signed copies; delivery disputes with no proof; the file incomplete. Correction: acknowledgments chased through the weekly read. Prevention: the signed copy as part of the despatch procedure, and the pending-acknowledgment read.</p><p><b>Error 4 — the cancelled truth.</b> Symptom: despatches that physically happened cancelled in the system to undo them; custody and cost misstated; the return lane bypassed. Correction: the cancellation reversed; the return documented as the physical event it was. Prevention: the correction-lane rule — cancellation only where goods did not leave; returns for goods that did.</p><p><b>Error 5 — returns straight to shelf.</b> Symptom: returned goods re-entering saleable stock without the condition decision; damaged or non-resaleable product sold onward. Correction: the affected stock quarantined and inspected. Prevention: the condition decision at receipt, with the pharmaceutical saleability ruling applied.</p><p><b>Error 6 — the unread returns pattern.</b> Symptom: returns processed individually with no aggregation; transit damage, picking errors, and supplier faults continuing because no read connects the cases. Correction: the returns and transit-damage reads compiled from the reason codes. Prevention: reason codes on every return and the monthly and quarterly reads.</p><p><b>Review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: complete the Delivery Note walk including addresses, honest dates, source warehouses, and identity on controlled rows; run picking, verification, despatch, and acknowledgment with their records; execute partial rows and waves under the one-document-per-destination rule and the tolerance settings; state the four postings of submission and the three correction lanes; process returns with the identity check, reason codes, the condition decision, and the commercial tie; and run the six delivery reads.</p><p><b>Module rule 5: the Delivery Note states what physically left — created from the order, identified at pick, verified by a second person, submitted at despatch, acknowledged at receipt, corrected only through the lane that matches what physically happened, and returned goods re-entering stock only through the condition decision.</b> Module 6 covers taxes, pricing rules, and invoicing preparation.</p>"),
]

